Emotion-Based Dog Filter

This is a webcam app that finds your face, guesses whether your expression is happy, sad or surprised, and puts a dog filter on you based on the guess. Happy gets the brown dog, sad gets the dalmatian, and surprise gets the sheepdog.

It only guesses the expression on the face. It can't tell what someone actually feels, and it doesn't detect dog emotions.

Use case

The main use is fun and entertainment. It works like the face filters on Snapchat or Instagram, except the filter is chosen by your expression instead of by you. It could be used in photo booths, in games, or as a feature in a video call app.

It is also a learning project. It shows a full computer vision pipeline from start to finish: face detection, training a classifier with transfer learning, testing it fairly on people it hasn't seen, and running it live on a webcam. Students can use it to understand how these systems are built and how they are tested.

It should not be used for anything serious, like judging someone's mood, interviews, or mental health. The model is trained on very little data and gets expressions wrong, and an expression on the face doesn't always match what a person feels.

How it works

The webcam frame goes through three steps. First, OpenCV's Haar cascade finds the face. Second, a ResNet-18 model (pretrained on ImageNet, then fine-tuned by me on JAFFE) picks Happy, Sad or Surprise. Third, the matching dog image gets pasted over the face.

To stop the filter from flickering, the app takes a vote over the last few predictions and smooths the face box.

Data and training

I used 92 images from the JAFFE dataset: 31 happy, 31 sad and 30 surprise. The images are split by person, so nobody appears in both training and testing. That gives 57 images for training, 17 for validation and 18 for testing.

Most of ResNet-18 is frozen. Only the last block and a new 3-class output layer are trained. Training runs on CPU for up to 40 epochs with early stopping.

Results

Validation accuracy was 94.12% (16/17), but that set was used to pick the best checkpoint, so it isn't the real score. On the held-out test set the accuracy was 66.67% (12/18).

On the test set, the 6 happy images were predicted as 1 happy, 3 sad and 2 surprise. The 6 sad images were predicted as 5 sad and 1 surprise. All 6 surprise images were predicted correctly.

So the model is good at surprise and okay at sad, but it misses most happy faces (1 out of 6 found).

Limitations

The test set is only 18 images from 2 people, so the score is noisy. One image changes it by about 5.6 points.

JAFFE is posed, grayscale photos, and a webcam gives live color video, so it may do worse in real use.

It always picks one of the three labels. There is no "unsure" option.

I haven't measured the speed (FPS).

Running it on Windows

1. Install Python 3.12.
2. Run setup_windows.bat.
3. Run test_webcam.bat to check the camera works.
4. Run run_filter.bat.
5. Press Q or Esc to quit.

To retrain, put the JAFFE images in src/data/external/jaffe and run prepare_dataset.py, then preprocess.py, then train.py, then evaluate.py, all from the src/training folder.

Files

The main app is src/step_8_dog_emotion_mask.py. The training code is in src/training. The other step_ files are earlier experiments and aren't used by the final app.

Future scope

More data. 92 images from JAFFE is very small. A bigger dataset with many more people, different ages, skin tones and lighting would give a more reliable score. People should still be kept separate between training and testing.

Better happy detection. The model only found 1 of 6 happy faces in the test. I would look at which images it got wrong and train with more varied happy examples.

More expressions. Right now there are only three. Angry, fear, disgust and neutral could be added, along with more masks.

Real webcam data. The model learned from posed grayscale photos but runs on live color video. Collecting and training on webcam images, with people's consent, should help close that gap.

An "unsure" option. At the moment the app always picks one of three labels, even for a face that fits none of them. A confidence threshold would let it show no filter when it isn't sure.

Better face detection. Haar cascades miss turned or poorly lit faces. A newer detector could handle those better.

Speed. I haven't measured the FPS yet. I would measure it and then try a lighter model or running on GPU.

More filters. Different animals, or filters that change with the strength of the expression, could make it more fun to use.

Fairness checks. Test whether the accuracy is the same across different groups of people before using it more widely.
